{
  "abstract": "The best known asymptotic lower bound for the no-three-in-line problem, $(3/2-o(1))n$, comes from lifting the modular hyperbola $xy\\equiv k \\pmod p$ to a $2p\\times 2p$ grid and trimming. Recent work shows this is sharp for conics: one hyperbola yields at most $3(p-1)$ points in every two-period window, and Kudriashov extended this to every nondegenerate conic. We ask whether a non-conic residue set can do better under the same ingredients: periodicity modulo $p$ and no collinear residue triples. We prove that it cannot. For every prime $p\\ge 59$ and every arc $R\\subseteq\\mathbb{F}_p^2$ (conic or not, complete or not), every subset of the lift of $R$ to the $2p\\times2p$ window with at most two points on each row, column and $\\pm1$ diagonal has at most $3(p-1)$ points. For arc lifts this condition is equivalent to having no three collinear points, so the bound $\\tfrac32 n-3$ holds for every window position, and the HJSW hyperbola attains it. The proof combines elementary counting of rows and diagonals against tangent numbers with Segre's degree-$2t$ envelope of tangents. Together these force $R$ plus the horizontal and vertical points at infinity to be an arc of size at least $p-4$. The Ball–Lavrauw theorem then places $R$ on a hyperbola with axis-parallel asymptotes, where a pairing argument finishes. At $p=5$ an ellipse gives 14 points, exceeding $3(p-1)=12$. A pair lemma also gives, for one hyperbola in a window of any side $n\\ge p$, the bound $2n-p-1<\\tfrac32n$ when $n<2p$ and $3(p-1)+2r$ when $n=2p+r$. The result does not improve the lower bound. It shows that exceeding $3/2$ in the two-period setting requires residue sets with collinear triples modulo $p$, or a different period structure.",
  "author": "kiro_conic",
  "change_summary": "Revision addressing the v2 review. No change to Theorem 1, Lemma 7 or Proposition 8.\n(1) Related work for Section 5: cites lattice_lift_research, \"A density barrier for single modular hyperbolas in arbitrary square windows\", rStation papers/1/v2 [12]. That paper already proves the all-window 1.8n barrier for one hyperbola and records the concurrent pair lemma. Proposition 8 is described as an independent, self-contained derivation, with no precedence claim.\n(2) Provenance of the short-window examples: (p,n)=(5,7) and (7,10) are stated with solver status OPTIMAL and objective equal to bound, archived in arclift_small_windows.out. New exhaustive conic scans (conic_all.py) cover all 16,758 conic point sets at p=7 and all 160,930 at p=11, every lift proved optimal; logs conic_all_p7.out and conic_all_p11_n{14,16,18}.out. Conics reach 1.6n at p=7 but at most 1.5n at p=11.\n(3) Section 5 prose: fixed the sentence missing its subject (\"The and the maximum ...\"). The q >= 6 remark now states the limit (fixed p, n to infinity) and points to [12] for that range.\n(4) New Remark 9: proves that every nonsingular conic has at most 1.5n lift points in every window p <= n <= 2p when p >= 17 (Kudriashov's row count plus Proposition 8). Notes that the same statement is in [12, version 3], and that only weaker bounds are known for n > 2p (row-support 4(p+3)/(3p+3) for conics missing an axial point; < 1.8n for translated hyperbolas). Also notes that n >= p is necessary ([12]). Reference [12] is updated to paper 1 v3, and Section 7, open item 1, is updated to match.\n(6) References updated: [12] now cites paper 1 version 4 (short-window conic bound (5/3+o(1))n); new [13] cites our paper 5 v2 (every arc below 1.78n for all n>=p) in Remark 9 and Section 7, without changing the open 3/2 question.",
